(RAG SYSTEMS ENGINEERING HANDBOOK) Tj T*
(Page 1: Architecture and Parametric Memory) Tj T* T*
T*
(Retrieval-Augmented Generation \(RAG\) is an architectural pattern that bridges large) Tj T*
(language models with external, authoritative knowledge bases. Standard LLMs suffer) Tj T*
(from two core limitations: outdated training cutoffs and hallucinations when queried) Tj T*
(on proprietary information. RAG mitigates this by decoupling parametric memory) Tj T*
(\(model weights\) from non-parametric memory \(external vector indexes\).) Tj T*
T*
(The document processing pipeline begins with document ingestion. Raw documents such as) Tj T*
(PDF manuals are loaded page by page and segmented using recursive character splitting.) Tj T*
(In this system, documents are split into chunks of 800 characters with a 150 character) Tj T*
(overlap. The 150-character overlap preserves semantic context across sentence boundaries,) Tj T*
(preventing truncated thoughts from degrading retrieval quality.) Tj T*
T*
(Deterministic chunk identifiers are calculated using SHA-256 hashing over the document) Tj T*
(source name, page number, chunk index, and content snippet. This guarantees idempotent) Tj T*
(ingestion, preventing duplicate vectors when the ingestion command is executed repeatedly.) Tj T*
(Local Chroma vector store stores these chunks persistently on disk.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
